WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.580 --> 00:00:04.430
It's fun to imagine what the world was like
around the time we each entered it.

00:00:04.430 --> 00:00:08.610
We were too young to remember anything, but
thanks to a detailed history of TV ratings,

00:00:08.610 --> 00:00:12.260
we know exactly which shows our parents were
bummed about missing because they were too

00:00:12.260 --> 00:00:13.580
busy giving birth to us.

00:00:13.580 --> 00:00:15.799
Or too tired from taking care of us.

00:00:15.799 --> 00:00:19.210
And they didn't have Hulu or Netflix to use
for catch-up purposes!

00:00:19.210 --> 00:00:24.570
Yeah, our parents were pretty much savages,
but what were they watching on TV?

00:00:24.570 --> 00:00:27.310
1972–1976: All in the Family

00:00:27.310 --> 00:00:32.630
The social upheaval of the late '60s gave
way to more social upheaval in the 70s, and

00:00:32.630 --> 00:00:37.340
lots of families' family time suddenly consisted
of parents arguing with their liberal offspring

00:00:37.340 --> 00:00:39.610
about various hot-button topics.

00:00:39.610 --> 00:00:42.000
All in the Family appealed to both sides.

00:00:42.000 --> 00:00:47.320
Most of the action centered around Carroll
O'Connor's Archie Bunker, a gruff but unexpectedly

00:00:47.320 --> 00:00:52.540
lovable bigot, who butted heads with his hippie
daughter and her husband, while wife Edith

00:00:52.540 --> 00:00:53.980
tried to keep the peace.

00:00:53.980 --> 00:00:59.120
All in the Family was one part sitcom, one
part public affairs discussion, one part moving

00:00:59.120 --> 00:01:03.720
drama, and it so resonated with Americans
that it stayed atop the ratings all the way

00:01:03.720 --> 00:01:06.000
into the disco era.

00:01:06.000 --> 00:01:08.110
1977: Happy Days

00:01:08.110 --> 00:01:13.120
Americans in the mid-'70s came down with a
bad case of nostalgia for the late '50s and

00:01:13.120 --> 00:01:14.120
early '60s.

00:01:14.120 --> 00:01:17.680
Enter: Happy Days, a throwback about the wholesome
Cunningham family.

00:01:17.680 --> 00:01:23.210
The Andy Griffith Show's child star and future
Oscar-winning director Ron Howard played wide-eyed

00:01:23.210 --> 00:01:27.700
high schooler Richie Cunningham, who hung
out at Arnold's Diner with friends like Arthur

00:01:27.700 --> 00:01:32.050
"Fonzie" Fonzarelli, who was quite possibly
the coolest character on television.

00:01:32.050 --> 00:01:36.560
He wore a leather jacket, rode a motorcycle,
could make a jukebox play by hitting it, and

00:01:36.560 --> 00:01:39.100
summoned young women with a snap of his fingers.

00:01:39.100 --> 00:01:40.040
They were different times.

00:01:47.020 --> 00:01:51.450
1978–1979: Laverne and Shirley

00:01:51.450 --> 00:01:56.700
A rare example of a spinoff doing was well
as the show it spun off from, Laverne and

00:01:56.700 --> 00:02:00.909
Shirley were first introduced in an episode
of Happy Days, then got their own sitcom.

00:02:00.909 --> 00:02:05.729
Penny Marshall's Laverne was edgy, Cindy Williams's
Shirley was a bit more optimistic.

00:02:05.729 --> 00:02:10.170
They were roommates, best friends, and co-workers
at the Shotz Brewery in the late '50s and

00:02:10.170 --> 00:02:11.170
early '60s.

00:02:11.170 --> 00:02:15.100
Bubbling with energy, Laverne and Shirley
balanced their time at the bottling plant

00:02:15.100 --> 00:02:19.810
with hanging out at Laverne's father's restaurant
and fending off the advances of their nerdy

00:02:19.810 --> 00:02:21.510
and obnoxious friends.

00:02:21.510 --> 00:02:25.871
1980, 1983, 1992–1994: 60 Minutes

00:02:25.871 --> 00:02:31.670
60 Minutes has been a part of the CBS lineup
since 1968, and the show's in-depth reporting

00:02:31.670 --> 00:02:34.230
made it a pioneer in TV news.

00:02:34.230 --> 00:02:39.231
It's produced a few moments of world-changing
journalism, such as Mike Wallace's 1996 story

00:02:39.231 --> 00:02:42.340
about tobacco industry whistleblower Jeffrey
Wigand.

00:02:42.340 --> 00:02:46.680
But perhaps 60 Minutes' most famous segment
has been the show-ending palate-cleanser "A

00:02:46.680 --> 00:02:50.320
Few Minutes with Andy Rooney," in which the
veteran humorously complained about whatever

00:02:50.320 --> 00:02:51.320
he felt like.

00:02:51.320 --> 00:02:57.960
"Not sleeping is not my problem"
"Justin Bieber Lady Gaga, and Usher…. "who?!"

00:02:57.960 --> 00:03:02.970
"When did bright colored plastic cows pigs
and rabbits get to be art?

00:03:02.970 --> 00:03:07.210
I don't like most of the stuff passing for
art and it's everywhere"

00:03:07.210 --> 00:03:11.000
1981, 1982 &amp; 1984: Dallas

00:03:11.000 --> 00:03:15.910
Primetime soaps were the biggest TV fad in
the late '70s and early '80s, and the biggest

00:03:15.910 --> 00:03:17.740
of them all was Dallas.

00:03:17.740 --> 00:03:22.599
It centered around the Ewing family and their
massive, Texas-based oil company, headed by

00:03:22.599 --> 00:03:25.520
Larry Hagman's downright evil J.R. Ewing.

00:03:25.520 --> 00:03:30.520
It also produced one of the most-watched TV
events ever: the two-part "Who Shot J.R.?"

00:03:30.520 --> 00:03:34.250
cliffhanger that got people tuning in the
following season.

00:03:34.250 --> 00:03:36.300
1985: Dynasty

00:03:36.300 --> 00:03:41.720
By 1985, the popularity of Dallas had diminished,
but audiences still wanted to see rich people

00:03:41.720 --> 00:03:43.910
behaving badly on primetime television.

00:03:43.910 --> 00:03:49.739
ABC happily obliged with Dynasty, starring
John Forsythe as tycoon Blake Carrington.

00:03:49.739 --> 00:03:55.000
Add in a much younger secretary-turned-wife,
as well as Joan Collins as Blake's ex-wife

00:03:55.000 --> 00:03:59.989
Alexis, and the show discovered exactly what
people wanted to see: Alexis and her new rival

00:03:59.989 --> 00:04:04.330
getting into arguments then slapping each
other around while wearing extremely expensive

00:04:04.330 --> 00:04:05.810
clothes.

00:04:05.810 --> 00:04:08.300
1986–1990: The Cosby Show

00:04:08.300 --> 00:04:14.319
With the recent allegations against Bill Cosby,
it's easy to forget his show was once so well-loved.

00:04:14.319 --> 00:04:20.200
Debuting on NBC in 1984 when the popularity
of sitcoms was fading, The Cosby Show was

00:04:20.200 --> 00:04:24.849
crafted to be a groundbreaking portrayal of
a black family something that was rarely seen

00:04:24.849 --> 00:04:26.159
on TV.

00:04:26.159 --> 00:04:30.599
The Cosby Show often played fast and loose
with sitcom standards and in doing so, changed

00:04:30.599 --> 00:04:32.280
viewers' expectations.

00:04:32.280 --> 00:04:37.440
Some episodes tackled heavy subject matter
while others had almost no plot at all, and

00:04:37.440 --> 00:04:41.990
just featured the family hanging out and showing
how great it was for a family to be together.

00:04:41.990 --> 00:04:46.729
It all came together to make The Cosby Show
an unstoppable juggernaut.

00:04:46.729 --> 00:04:48.659
1990: Roseanne

00:04:48.659 --> 00:04:53.060
The Cosby Show wasn't alone as the most-viewed
show on TV in 1990.

00:04:53.060 --> 00:04:58.979
It also has to contend with Roseanne, another
show that broke new ground in television representation.

00:04:58.979 --> 00:05:03.379
Rarely had anyone attempted a show about a
blue-collar Midwestern family with two working

00:05:03.379 --> 00:05:04.379
parents.

00:05:04.379 --> 00:05:09.309
But Roseanne was also the anti-Cosby Show,
built around a family's financial struggles,

00:05:09.309 --> 00:05:14.060
messy lives, and real-world problems that
never resolved at the end of an episode.

00:05:14.060 --> 00:05:17.099
Through it all, they still managed to stay
a close knit family.

00:05:17.099 --> 00:05:22.779
It was such an indelible portrait of a particular
kind of American family that ABC revived Roseanne

00:05:22.779 --> 00:05:28.689
for a short-lived 2018 run, more than 20 years
after it initially went off the air.

00:05:28.689 --> 00:05:30.800
1991: Cheers

00:05:30.800 --> 00:05:35.759
Cheers was so different from other contemporary
sitcoms that when it debuted in 1982, it was

00:05:35.759 --> 00:05:39.520
nearly canceled, before taking home some Emmys
in 1983.

00:05:39.520 --> 00:05:44.560
By 1989, everyone was invested in what became
the nation's favorite neighborhood bar and

00:05:44.560 --> 00:05:46.980
the cast of characters who frequented it.

00:05:52.970 --> 00:05:58.420
It was so popular, in fact, that it led to
the also-successful spin-off, Frasier, launched

00:05:58.430 --> 00:06:04.180
the career of Woody Harrelson, and gets a
shout-out in scores of pop culture references.

00:06:04.180 --> 00:06:06.820
1995 &amp; 1998: Seinfeld

00:06:06.820 --> 00:06:11.900
Finally, after years of shows about things,
along came a show about nothing.

00:06:11.900 --> 00:06:16.569
Except that Seinfeld was about something the
hilariously dark and selfish impulses of some

00:06:16.569 --> 00:06:17.839
nasty New Yorkers.

00:06:17.839 --> 00:06:23.039
Based on Jerry Seinfeld's scattered observational
comedy, it turned into one of the most intricately

00:06:23.039 --> 00:06:25.599
written and inventive sitcoms ever made.

00:06:25.599 --> 00:06:30.830
It also up-ended the traditional sitcom formula
by featuring bizarre, classic moments, like

00:06:30.830 --> 00:06:35.930
ordering from the Soup Nazi and messing up
an operation by dropping a Junior Mint.

00:06:35.930 --> 00:06:39.719
Americans loved the cleverly written storylines
that somehow all connected at the end, and

00:06:39.719 --> 00:06:42.280
it beat everything else on the air.

00:06:42.280 --> 00:06:46.060
1996–1997 &amp; 1999: ER

00:06:46.060 --> 00:06:51.330
The fast-paced, ultra-realistic drama ER marked
another shift in viewer tastes.

00:06:51.330 --> 00:06:55.740
Breaking up the life-and-death tension of
a big-city emergency room were scenes involving

00:06:55.740 --> 00:07:00.169
the doctors' personal lives; moments that
were just as captivating thanks to stars like

00:07:00.169 --> 00:07:03.800
George Clooney, Julianna Margulies, and Noah
Wyle.

00:07:03.800 --> 00:07:09.310
ER was a mainstay of NBC's schedule for 15
seasons, and at times, it was the most watched

00:07:09.310 --> 00:07:10.169
show on TV.

